  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "zend_hash_reverse_apply",
    "file": "php-src/Zend/zend_hash.c",
    "description": "`zend_hash_reverse_apply()` iterates buckets using a cached `Bucket *p = ht->arData + idx`, then calls `apply_func(&p->val)`. During shutdown, that callback path can run user destructors which mutate `$GLOBALS` and trigger `zend_hash_add_new()` -> `zend_hash_do_resize()`, reallocating/freing `ht->arData`. After returning, `zend_hash_reverse_apply()` still uses the stale `p` pointer and passes it to `_zend_hash_del_el()`, which dereferences freed memory (`p->h` / `p->key`), causing heap-use-after-free. Root cause is missing iterator/pointer invalidation handling for table resize/reentrancy in `zend_hash_reverse_apply` (not just the deref site).",
    "conditions": "Trigger requires reentrant mutation of the same HashTable while `zend_hash_reverse_apply()` is walking it (here, request shutdown destructor traversal). A destructor invoked from element deletion must insert enough entries into `$GLOBALS` to force hash resize (`zend_hash_do_resize` frees old bucket storage). Then reverse-apply continues with pre-resize bucket pointer and performs remove path (`ZEND_HASH_APPLY_REMOVE`), leading to UAF in `_zend_hash_del_el`."
  },
